From 619ec672020452630083ffc17c6b5485d6222680 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Piyush Jena <jepiyush@amazon.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 17:56:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] libnvme: resolve symlinks of the device argument

nvme-cli 3.x opens the device given on the command line through
libnvme_open(). __libnvme_transport_handle_open_direct() takes only the
basename of the argument, requires it to be nvme<N>, nvme<N>n<M> or
ng<N>n<M>, and then opens /dev/<basename>. The path itself is never
looked at, so a udev symlink to the device node is rejected with
EINVAL: the basename of /dev/disk/by-ebs-id/xvdc is "xvdc", and the
one of /dev/disk/by-id/nvme-Amazon_Elastic_Block_Store_vol... is not
nvme<N> either. nvme-cli 2.x opened the given path itself and accepted
any path to an NVMe character or block device, including symlinks.

When the argument contains a '/', resolve it with realpath() and take
the device name from the resolved path. If realpath() fails, use the
argument as before, so plain device names such as nvme0n1 and paths
that don't exist behave exactly as they did.

Signed-off-by: Piyush Jena <jepiyush@amazon.com>
---
 libnvme/src/nvme/lib-linux.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/libnvme/src/nvme/lib-linux.c b/libnvme/src/nvme/lib-linux.c
index 2582176..54c3292 100644
--- a/libnvme/src/nvme/lib-linux.c
+++ b/libnvme/src/nvme/lib-linux.c
@@ -8,6 +8,8 @@
  */
 
 #include <fcntl.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
+#include <string.h>
 #include <sys/ioctl.h>
 
 #include <libnvme.h>
@@ -25,11 +27,19 @@ static int __libnvme_transport_handle_open_direct(
 		int flags)
 {
 	__cleanup_free char *path = NULL;
+	__cleanup_free char *real = NULL;
 	char *name;
 	int ret, id, ns;
 	bool c = true;
 
-	name = shr_basename(devname);
+	/*
+	 * Follow symlinks such as /dev/disk/by-id/... to the device node
+	 * they point to; its name is what has to match below.
+	 */
+	if (strchr(devname, '/'))
+		real = realpath(devname, NULL);
+
+	name = shr_basename(real ? real : devname);
 
 	hdl->type = LIBNVME_TRANSPORT_HANDLE_TYPE_DIRECT;
 
